{"sections":[],"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Using-SwiftUsd-from-C++","title":"Using SwiftUsd from C++","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxInteropMemorySafety","doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxMacros"]}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swiftusd\/gettingstartedincplusplus"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]},{"paths":["\/documentation\/swiftusd\/gettingstartedincplusplus"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"occ"}]}],"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","modules":[{"name":"OpenUSD"}],"role":"article","title":"Getting Started In C++"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Getting Started","anchor":"Getting-Started"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Before you can start using SwiftUsd, you need to add it as a dependency to your Xcode project or Swift Package, and then configure a few build settings."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"style":"note","name":"Note","type":"aside","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Despite the fact that SwiftUsd is a Swift Package, you can use it in a pure-C++ Xcode project\/target, as well as in a pure-C++ Swift Package target."}]}]},{"style":"important","name":"Important","type":"aside","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swift Package targets cannot mix Swift and C++ code in a single target. "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/github.com\/swiftlang\/swift-package-manager\/issues\/8945","isActive":true}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To do so, follow the steps in "},{"overridingTitle":"Getting Started for Swift","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/GettingStarted#Getting-Started","overridingTitleInlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Getting Started for Swift"}],"type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":". If you’re using a pure-C++ target, you don’t need to change the "},{"code":"C